===== /candidate/PROOF.md =====
     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	# What is proven
     4	
     5	Under the supplied MPY semantics, for every finite `ValSeq INPUT` satisfying
     6	`allInts(INPUT)`, loading the translated `remove_duplicates` definition and
     7	calling it on `list(INPUT)` reaches normal completion with:
     8	
     9	- result value `ref(0)`;
    10	- heap entry `0 |-> list(rdAcc(.ValSeq, INPUT, INPUT))`;
    11	- `NoExc` and exit code `0`; and
    12	- the module environment, empty call stack, and allocation counters in the
    13	  final states stated by `SPEC.remove-duplicates`.
    14	
    15	This is a partial-correctness reachability theorem. The Kit workflow does not
    16	formally establish termination.
    17	
    18	`rdAcc` is the order-preserving filter defined by:
    19	
    20	```text
    21	rdAcc(acc, [], all) = acc
    22	rdAcc(acc, v :: rest, all) =
    23	  rdAcc(acc ++ [v], rest, all)  if count(all, v) == 1
    24	  rdAcc(acc,        rest, all)  otherwise
    25	```
    26	
    27	Consequently, from an empty accumulator it contains exactly the original input
    28	integers whose total input multiplicity is one, in their original order.
    29	
    30	# Formal claims
    31	
    32	`SPEC.remove-duplicates-loop` is the loop invariant. At a loop head it relates:
    33	
    34	- `REST`, the unprocessed input suffix;
    35	- `ACC`, the current contents of the result-list heap object;
    36	- `ALL`, the unchanged complete input used by `list.count`; and
    37	- the final heap content `rdAcc(ACC, REST, ALL)`.
    38	
    39	It quantifies over the same arbitrary continuation admitted by its `<k> ...`
    40	frame. It observes the exact local bindings for `numbers` and `result`, permits
    41	only the intentionally unobserved final value of local `number` to be
    42	existential, and preserves all framed configuration cells.
    43	
    44	`SPEC.remove-duplicates` starts with the complete translated module and an
    45	initial MPY configuration. It executes module loading, exact closure creation,
    46	argument binding, the function body, the loop, return, and frame cleanup.
    47	
    48	# Proof-extension inventory
    49	
    50	## `allInts`
    51	
    52	- **Class:** Definitional summary.
    53	- **Semantic role:** Precondition predicate only; it does not rewrite `<k>` or
    54	  replace execution.
    55	- **Domain:** Every `ValSeq`.
    56	- **Matched context / state footprint:** Pure term context; reads and writes no
    57	  configuration cells.
    58	- **Equations:** Empty and `vCons` cases are exhaustive and disjoint. Recursion
    59	  strictly descends through the tail.
    60	- **Value influence:** Restricts both claims to the prompt's `List[int]`
    61	  domain.
    62	- **Justification:** `true` on empty and `isInt(head) and allInts(tail)` on a
    63	  nonempty sequence.
    64	- **Dependents:** Both claims.
    65	
    66	## `rdAcc`
    67	
    68	- **Class:** Definitional summary.
    69	- **Semantic role:** Names the mathematical output without replacing any
    70	  program term.
    71	- **Domain:** Every accumulator, remaining sequence, and original sequence of
    72	  sort `ValSeq`; the claims use it only under `allInts`.
    73	- **Matched context / state footprint:** Pure term context; reads and writes no
    74	  configuration cells.
    75	- **Equations:** Empty and `vCons` cases are exhaustive and disjoint. The
    76	  nonempty case contains one total Boolean conditional. Recursion strictly
    77	  descends through `REST`.
    78	- **Value influence:** Determines the claimed final result-list contents.
    79	- **Value justification:** Its equation uses the supplied, defined
    80	  `cntOccVS(ALL, V)` and appends `V` exactly when that count is one.
    81	- **Dependents:** The loop and entry claims.
    82	
    83	## `SPEC.remove-duplicates-loop`
    84	
    85	- **Class:** Derived lemma / auxiliary reachability claim.
    86	- **Semantic role:** Machine-checked fixed-semantics execution theorem used as
    87	  the loop circularity; it is not an ordinary operational rule.
    88	- **Domain:** `allInts(REST) andBool allInts(ALL)`, arbitrary result prefix
    89	  `ACC`, heap location `H`, environment `L`, and current local `number`.
    90	- **Matched context:** The exact `#loop` term and exact loop body, an arbitrary
    91	  continuation quantified by the claim itself, the three relevant local
    92	  bindings, the result heap object, and framed remaining cells.
    93	- **Context containment:** The theorem proves the same framed continuation and
    94	  configuration domain that the circularity accepts; it does not generalize an
    95	  exact-suffix theorem to a wider suffix.
    96	- **State footprint:** Reads `numbers`, `result`, `number`, and heap `H`; writes
    97	  local `number` and heap `H`; preserves the input, environment, continuation,
    98	  and every other framed cell.
    99	- **Value influence:** Establishes the exact final result-list value.
   100	- **Justification:** Fixed semantics executes the base case and one inductive
   101	  iteration; recursive loop heads close coinductively. The focused proof printed
   102	  `#Top`.
   103	- **Control/value validation:** `spec-body-mutation.k` changes the comparison
   104	  from count one to count two while retaining the original destination. It is
   105	  rejected with a count-one residual.
   106	- **Dependents:** `SPEC.remove-duplicates`.
   107	
   108	There are no proof-local operational bridges, opaque result oracles, trusted
   109	primitives, priority rules, concrete rules, or simplification lemmas.
   110	
